# nrz-cas · `guide:cas:overview`

`nrz-cas` constructs exact mathematical graphs, checks conditional identity witnesses, searches bounded equality routes and produces scalar derivatives, Jacobians and rational square-free layers with explicit sorts, contexts, binder scopes, resource budgets and original source occurrences. Native cases add complete source inspection, reached-region elaboration, local agreement, merge, ownership and bounded common refinement to semantic headers, exact region judgments, qualified local contexts and typed portable replay, following `docs/design/cas-piecewise-design.md`. It has no consumer yet and introduces no physical units or numerical evaluator.

The root exports `Cas`, handles/references, `Rational` and structured faults. Construction lives in `syntax`, domains/headers/contexts in `domain`, occurrence coverage in `source`, tasks/results/search in `request`, judgments/witnesses in `proof`, and accounting in `limits`. A `Request` pairs each occurrence root with its matching receipt in `CoveredInput` and retains explicit witness premises. The retained `Draft::Request` boxes its `Limits` payload to bound draft size; the executable `Request` retains value-owned limits.

The `solving` facade adds exact coordinate products, tuples, coefficient algebras and values, sparse polynomials, extension levels, coefficient maps, root relations and joint selections, solution branches and sets, original problems and local germs. `Draft::Solve` admits syntax atomically; `Cas::solve_view` borrows graph-owned payloads and checked handles. Constructors never prove membership, existence, field structure or completeness. Product regions retain coordinate order; the existing interval checkers refuse them. Rational-function admission independently checks coprime multivariate numerator and denominator polynomials over Q or Q(i), with a monic denominator. Recursive content and primitive-part gcd calculations preserve the fixed formal variable order; coprimality does not assert a multivariate Bézout identity to one. Noncanonical pairs refuse atomically.

`Task::Solve(Box<SolveSpec>)` names the original problem, candidate or complete goal, exact presentation and `PreserveOriginal` exclusions. The covered predicate must agree exactly with that problem. Solving producers remain unavailable, so a valid solve returns `Unresolved` with `CoefficientDomainUnavailable`. `SolveAnswer` separates sealed facts, optional coverage and unresolved regions; construction cannot manufacture a successful answer. `Statement::Solve` and `Certificate::Solve` retain closed typed payloads for subsequent replay checkers. The sole current `NRZCAS03` witness format includes the typed objects, scalar attachments and closed solving payloads; unavailable theorem schemas still refuse replay.

The numeric leaf now supplies exact Gaussian-rational arithmetic over `Complex<Rational>` through crate-private fallible operations. `solving::capabilities()` reports that leaf as available. Addition, subtraction, multiplication, inverse, division, conjugation, components, norm, equality and zero tests share the caller’s budget; composite operations preflight their complete scalar sequence, including intermediate sizes, temporary storage, limb work and conservative gcd reservations. The existing arithmetic-step counter counts scalar dependency calls; work records their limb costs. Numeric availability alone enables no solving theorem or producer.

The coefficient leaf supplies a sealed, fallible arithmetic interface over Q, Q(i) and rational functions. Sparse operations reserve uncancelled term bounds, charge exponent comparisons and recursively meter scalar operations; normalization uses deterministic merge sorting and exact polynomial factor reconstruction. Arithmetic results and proposed inverse evidence remain unsealed. A formally nonzero parameter numerator is not a unit on every parameter fiber: regional inversion retains a nonvanishing obligation unless exact scoped evidence decides it. Finite exact specialization checks every named localization denominator and the evaluated value’s denominator, and can map a nonzero formal parameter to zero. Injective constant inclusions and distinct independent-variable renamings are separate map modes. The independent coefficient identity checker validates explicit maps, denominator premises and exact polynomial differences; branch-sensitive or unsupported expressions refuse.

Scoped equality predicates can justify coefficient relation multiples through `ScopedCoefficientRelation`: an exact earlier premise, two independently matched polynomial endpoints and an explicit ordered root-term interpretation. Formal polynomial slots remain distinct from scalar atoms. An optional `RootRelation` anchor requests existential discharge only for a complete Vieta system over a complex bound frame, with independent root-existence evidence for the exact polynomial, degree, leading coefficient and region. The checker reconstructs every elementary symmetric equation and refuses incomplete systems or conclusions, maps, regions and enclosing assumptions that still depend on the formal roots. This removes only the qualified Vieta hypotheses; other conditions and all proof dependencies remain. A local formal-root frame may close that discharged, root-independent identity into its exact original unscoped parent. Closure checks a bounded structural erasure of the complex binder, preserves every denominator and retained condition, and keeps the expanded identity, Vieta relations and independent existence proof reachable. The result is an ordinary coefficient equality usable by later unscoped rules. Ordinary scoped relations retain their hypotheses, and relations justified by earlier coefficient equalities keep their existing checking route. No formal root enters the computational tower. Bound contexts require every predicate and local restriction to match the exact region scope. Their executable proof route is limited to scoped coefficient identities, hypothesis introduction and closure, region construction, and independently checked root-independent unit/existence support. Scalar proof/search, automatic request producers and all other native rules refuse these contexts with `UnsupportedFragment`; empty-scope behavior is unchanged.

Coefficient identities can compare a polynomial or value over a source quotient with its image in another coefficient domain through an explicit specialization map. Replay checks the source defining polynomial at the exact target generator image, transports source coefficients, and reads target coefficients directly. Both sides retain the same ordered formal variable frame, exact region and denominator evidence; an unused slot still belongs to that frame. The finite modulus check establishes a homomorphism and does not assert injectivity.

Exact zero and nonzero guards on the validated evaluation region now decide the matching canonical parameter polynomial. The checker follows guard bases and intersections, compares full headers and ordered declaration keys, and accepts total polynomial expressions or polynomial coefficient literals with canonical parameter maps. A fresh zero/nonzero split therefore permits inversion only on its nonzero cell, without adding a contextual hypothesis. Proved nonzero guards and scoped nonzero premises are closed under products and positive powers by repeated exact polynomial division down to a nonzero ground constant. Each successful division strictly lowers total degree, uses the existing coefficient and arithmetic limits, and rejects a nonzero remainder; overlapping proved factors retain alternative division routes. Thus the nonzero cell for a also defines 1/a² and 1/a³, while a product a·b requires both factors to be proved nonzero. No factorization or nonvanishing guess from polynomial spelling is added. Independent identity and denominator replay use this same checked closure; exact zero decisions, actual value denominators and finite localization obligations remain mandatory. Unions, arbitrary positivity and partial guard arithmetic do not become general region decisions.

The tower leaf enables `coeff.identity`, `extension.unit` and `extension.split` (tags 0, 2 and 3). Monic finite quotients use parent arithmetic followed by descending reduction, and Euclidean inversion returns a unit, zero, a nonvanishing obligation or a component split. An earlier parent split takes precedence over a child computation. Replay independently verifies gcd divisibilities, Bézout identities and both inverse products. Component covers additionally reconstruct the proper factor product, coprimality, derivative-unit square-freeness, inductive parent reducedness and the actual child-generator projections. Component staging rebuilds dependent coefficient domains, polynomials and values from parent to child on both branches in one private transaction. Selection transport preserves the same ordered joint predicate and every unchanged relation slot on both children; mismatched constraints or a root sent to the wrong factor refuse. No quotient is called a field merely because its defining polynomial is square-free.

`extension.adjoin` (tag 1) now checks an exact leading-unit premise and a well-defined scalar coefficient map before asserting root existence. Complex FTA and algebraic closure of C support unconstrained root relations/selections and checked degree-decreasing splitting chains. Primitive-unity existence reconstructs the cyclotomic polynomial from the divisor product of Xⁿ−1; no numerical root or orientation is selected. Real odd binomials admit real roots, while positive/nonnegative real square-root selections require exact radicand signs or matching scoped sign premises. A selection’s principal or isolated metadata cannot supply existence or uniqueness. Caller-named fields remain unavailable without a separately specified closure interpretation.

`orbit.cover` (tag 14) independently checks finite polynomial orbits under declared sort-preserving permutation bijections. Replay compares every action using exact coefficient arithmetic, rejects duplicate normalized members, checks closure and follows a reaching word for every member. The conclusion names the generated group only; full symmetric invariance requires the adjacent-transposition generators. Orbit actions, monomial visits and member peaks consume their separate budgets. Missing members, false tables and unreachable additions refuse atomically, and portable orbit witnesses replay in an empty store.

The symmetric leaf generates cyclic-character powers up to their computed character order, then partition seeds in restricted-growth-string order. It enumerates full adjacent-transposition orbits and expands only strictly smaller resolvents. Leading-lex subtraction computes every invariant coefficient in elementary symmetric functions, without a formula cache. Derived recipes retain explicit root and parameter images, coefficient maps and map hypotheses; `coeff.identity` independently checks the nested expansion and simultaneous coefficient substitution. Symmetry, expansion and substitution evidence is published together only after replay, with no implied solution coverage.

Integer and rational perfect-power tests separate the sign and positive-order checks from magnitude arithmetic. Bounded `num_integer` primitive roots seed an explicitly metered binary refinement for larger integers; exact repeated squaring verifies every nontrivial candidate. Negative even radicands return no real root. Rational testing requires exact roots of both reduced components. Conservative preflight may refuse a calculation before its reduced result reaches the component ceiling.

`SOLVE_SCHEMAS` publishes the 18 stable certificate subtype identities; `active_schema_bitmap()` and `SearchPolicy::active_schema_bitmap()` expose the separate executable capability. The bitmap belongs to search state keys and response audits, while mathematical witnesses carry only their exact used-rule manifest. Policy identity lookup uses a dedicated `(key, version)` index and never scans unrelated graph records.

The elementary leaf enables `solve.member`, `solve.substitute` and `solve.split` (tags 4–6). Membership simultaneously substitutes the ordered unknown tuple into the original predicate, solution domain and every retained original denominator. Reversible coordinate changes independently check both directions, strict domains and inverse compositions. Total zero/nonzero and real sign partitions require definedness before asserting a cover; regional coverage assembly retains every supplied branch. All used hypotheses and residual guards remain visible. These supplied-certificate checks do not enable automatic solving requests.

The supplied elementary checks also enable exact binomial order and coverage, constructive root-selection refinement, and product completeness (tags 7, 8 and 10). Positive factor multiplicities reconstruct the full original polynomial; excluded roots must violate a retained original denominator. Complete products cover the zero-radicand singleton and nonzero constant empty set. Degree-generic unsealed recipes strip specialized zero leading coefficients and translate away the subleading term, deriving quadratics through the same binomial path. Root values require sealed existence for their exact selection. Finite covers transport through the checked inverse maps; no numerical root sampling or radical branch convention supplies a theorem.

The supplied linear and family checks enable tags 9 and 16. Reversible row operations reconstruct the full matrix, nonzero echelon pivots certify a triangular minor, and all remaining rows must vanish for the rank upper bound. A pivot or scale may use an earlier checked scalar inverse identity with the same normalized pivot, exact coefficient map, context and region; both endpoints remain defined and their product is checked through the root relations. Canonically embedded nonzero ground constants in fractions and constant tower vectors are units, but formal parameter numerators and arbitrary quotient representatives are not. Affine systems retain their original equations and augmented ranks. Binomial coverage and linear-system claims must stay inside the original coefficient region, as do product covers, cover elimination, transport and family certificates. Family membership substitutes arbitrary bound parameters; coverage additionally checks an exact reverse-space recovery map, its full domain and the identity on every original solution. The zero polynomial therefore admits its whole-domain identity family. Automatic linear-family production remains separate.

Tags 11–13 add derivative multiplicities, standard complex-conjugation expression induction and finite real restriction. Derivative mode checks every derivative through the first nonzero order; square-free-layer multiplicity certificates remain unsupported. Conjugation never identifies independent root choices. Real restriction uses mandatory ordered coefficient, declaration, region and used-condition transport across the real and complex headers. It checks both coefficient covers, classifies every retained and rejected branch on its exact region, preserves linked root selections, and transports used hypotheses into proved target facts or explicit target residuals. Distinct display names and distinct coefficient-domain handles are permitted only through the checked correspondence. The whole-domain zero-polynomial case restricts the identity family, retaining all real preimages. Radical Presentation checks nested binomial/root-of-unity provenance and rejects unresolved general algebraic placeholders. A binomial radical requires its specialized leading coefficient to be proved nonzero on the claimed region; constructive root existence on a degree-degenerate stratum is insufficient. ExactFamily presentations require image or empty branches, and explicit family parameters remain bound. A parent Presentation may name earlier closed child Presentation statements through compatibility slots. Composition requires the same original problem and requested form, exact unchanged branch identities, containment of each child region, and a separate complete Coverage theorem for the exact combined set; every target branch must be supplied. This composes recursively across coefficient splits without reopening or widening leaf root and family proofs. These exact supplied-certificate capabilities do not enable an automatic solving request.

Complex component checking now interprets returned `Fraction` and reduced `Tower` literals through their checked scalar maps, preserving every denominator, parent map and actual selected root slot. Constructive `root.select` witnesses check real-component sign constraints, conjugation predicates and norm products on the exact coefficient region. `complex.conjugate` consumes those checked constraints and earlier exact `RealValues` or scalar equality premises named by its relation and fixed-field slots. Each referenced fact is interpreted once for the whole application; unrelated earlier scalar equalities are not expanded into component equations. Thus real polynomial coefficients and selection metadata alone supply no realness. Rational quadratics with negative discriminant have their real component and strictly nonzero imaginary square checked by completing the square. Real restriction uses the transported coefficient parameters, proves nonrealness of rejected values, and accepts a changed retained presentation only through a checked scalar equality on the same stratum. Supplied cubic fixtures reconstruct the full products for x³ + 6x − 7 and Bombelli’s x³ − 15x − 4, then replay their realness and conjugation claims in fresh stores. No certificate payload, wire tag or automatic producer is added.

The 21 solving ceilings have matching `BudgetKind`, `LimitsOverride` and `BudgetUsage` fields. `Budget::solving` fixes each resource's cumulative or peak counting rule and retains attempted usage on refusal. Admission checks denominator factors before deduplication, staged maps/branches, tower depth and degree products, family dimensions and selection slots. `Limits::blind_cubic()` constructs exactly the finite profile pinned in the solving design's blind-cubic overrides table; it changes no other defaults and never retries with higher ceilings. Store and request limits must both admit the explicit profile.

**Definition (Scoped region capability)** · `def:cas:scoped-regions`

Headers separate immutable declarations from assumptions. `Core` is space-free; `Extend` adds fresh declarations referring only to earlier spaces and cannot shadow existing keys. Regions use exact ambient, empty, guard-true, guard-false, meet, union and difference constructors. The checker decides rational affine comparisons and integer cells, preserving singleton endpoints and strict definedness; an undefined guard is neither true nor false. Real distribution spaces currently require a nonempty connected open interval and the `CompactSmooth` test family.

A local context records its parent, exact admissible meet and qualified frame slots. Hypothesis use names its introducing context and slot; siblings cannot borrow that identity. Closure removes only used local assumptions, using supplied parent evidence or retaining a guarded residual on the child region. Restriction requires an explicit checked subset premise. Predicate facts are checked on exact nonempty regions; empty-region facts hold vacuously without evaluating their predicate. An empty requested function domain refuses.

`source::ordered_cases` and `source::case_literal` build complete original forms and distinct occurrences atomically. `describe_syntax` exposes constructor payloads and typed dependency roles for expressions, conditions, regions, arms, boundaries, spaces and source forms. Original walks include every selector and branch, including inactive partial expressions. `ElaborateCases` requires an original receipt and checks the ordered reached-region recurrence; an undefined reached guard never falls through.

The five case schemas reconstruct exact domains, local selection, overlap compatibility and complete congruence. Rational polynomial identities are checked on real open cells and values are evaluated exactly at rational singletons. `Merge` records domain proofs, a cover, local equalities and closure, preserving both definedness directions. `Boundary` with `Ownership` records function-specific `Present(value)` or certified `Absent`; missing evidence remains `PendingPremise`. `Coverage` proves definedness on the requested region, distinguishes `UnknownTotality` from a proved hole, and preserves unsupported predicates as blockers. General integer-cell agreement beyond polynomial identity or exact singleton evaluation remains pending.

Native `Simplify` schedules common-refinement pairs lazily on the cost and progress queues. Empty pairs consume the shared refinement allowance, nested jobs share the same ledger, positive growth is charged, and each complete result retains a checked cover. Boundary calculus adds finite selector partitions, polynomial side jets, classical cell and point derivatives, witnessed nonexistence, and transparent named definitions. Distribution calculus adds checked regular embeddings, finite jump derivatives, delta actions and polynomial smooth multipliers in an exact declared space.

**Definition (Certified boundary calculus)** · `def:cas:boundary-calculus`

`Task::Boundary` names its real coordinate and point. `Jets { order }` requests traces from zero through that order; `Jump { order }` requests the right-minus-left trace difference; `Derivative { order }` names a positive iterated classical derivative order. Complete finite partition evidence reconstructs every reachable case selector and requested-domain endpoint. Geometry alone supplies no analytic regularity: each jet additionally requires total local equality to a rational polynomial and an independently checked incident open neighborhood.

`jet.polynomial` checks factorial-weighted shifted coefficients, with separate closed partition, trace-projection and jump-introduction modes. The derived `jet.jump` recipe records both projections and exact subtraction. `diff.case-interior` transports an existing scalar derivative through local equality on an open cell; its assembly mode checks all cells of the claimed derivative region. `diff.case-boundary` first compares both function traces with actual function-specific ownership, then compares slopes. Missing ownership or a side stays pending. Thus equal branch slopes do not rescue J's jump.

`Cas::named_case` builds `NamedCase { name, coordinate, parameter }` with a retained canonical case operand. The checked case machinery unfolds abs, sign, ramp and a step whose rational parameter is its value at zero. Ordinary coordinate substitution must use an expanded definition; `rename_axes` transports the coordinate and definition together. Names grant no primitive shortcut.

`SearchResult::payload` boxes typed calculus results. A sealed `DerivativeMap` retains the complete selector partition, all replayed facts, pending cells, a separate derivative theorem on the union of successful cells, and an optional complete requested-domain derivative theorem. Higher orders consume the actual previous derivative, including owned point values. Abs and ramp have side derivatives −1/+1 and 0/1 with witnessed nonexistence at zero. J has owned value 1, traces 0/1 and slopes 0/0, so its derivative fails there. Q has first derivative zero at zero and second-derivative traces 2/4, proving second-derivative nonexistence.

**Definition (Finite split calculus)** · `def:cas:split-calculus`

`Task::Integrate(Box<SplitRange>)` and `Task::Sum(Box<SplitRange>)` consume one covered function occurrence. Bounds are exact finite rationals, and sums require ordered integer endpoints. `Statement::Split` retains the coordinate, full closed interval, original orientation and exact result. Integrals verify every polynomial cell and its antiderivative, with explicit owned or absent cut values; all finite-valued point extensions have the same integral. Sums verify a disjoint inclusive integer-range cover and polynomial forward differences, including every singleton. Improper convergence, symbolic bounds and infinite sums remain outside this fragment.

The independent `integral.case-split` and `sum.case-split` checkers have semantic version 1 and logical weights 8 and 4. `split_certificate` exposes checked geometry and endpoint evidence. Producers use coefficient recurrences and Horner evaluation; replay uses derivative/composition identities and explicit powers. Both share the existing metered partition cache and apply the case, region, boundary, polynomial and wire ceilings.

**Definition (Standalone limits and side series)** · `def:cas:germ-calculus`

`Task::Limit(Box<GermAt>)` uses checked polynomial side jets and trace projections on nonempty punctured neighborhoods. A two-sided request requires equal limits; sign therefore has limits −1 and 1 and a refused two-sided result. `Task::Series(Box<SeriesAt>)` retains centre, side, order and formal/analytic mode. Its exact remainder witnesses f(a+h) = P(h) + h^(n+1)R(h); ramp on the right at zero has coefficients `[0, 1, 0]` at order two and zero remainder.

Formal side claims concern a punctured domain. Ordinary two-sided Taylor claims require matching coefficients and the actual centre value. Analytic mode additionally requires both full polynomial germs, including their remainders, to agree across that centre. This checks polynomial analyticity and supplies no general convergence theorem. `limit.case-restrict` and `series.case-restrict` have semantic version 1 and weights 2 and 4. All trace and remainder dependencies survive canonical expected replay, with the existing jet budgets and partition cache.

**Definition (Checked distribution calculus)** · `def:cas:distribution-calculus`

`EmbedRegular { space }` certifies finite polynomial cells before constructing a regular distribution. `DistributionStatement` groups regularity, embedding, equality, derivative, pairing and supported-action judgments under `Statement::Distribution`. Every judgment retains its exact real coordinate, open interval and test family, including scalar-valued pairing results. Regular representatives may differ at certified finite points; their pointwise ownership remains distinct.

The first nine distribution schemas are `dist.regular`, `dist.ae`, `dist.linear`, `dist.derivative`, `dist.delta-pair`, `dist.smooth`, `dist.delta-multiply`, `dist.support-action` and `dist.jump`. The jump checker independently compares polynomial derivatives and right-minus-left traces on the complete partition. Higher derivatives repeat the first-order rule and increment existing delta orders. Ordinary declared tests produce symbolic derivative evaluations; `PairNearSupport` instead requires a checked finite delta support and a polynomial germ on an explicit neighborhood. It evaluates `PairNearSupport(δ₂, x² + 3) = 7` and `PairNearSupport(δ′₂, x² + 3) = −4` without classifying a polynomial as a compactly supported test.

The derived integration-by-parts, delta-primitive and classical-bridge recipes retain primitive expansions and add no registry keys. Named and expanded routes enter both queues before completed-result dominance. Native equality shortcuts retain replayable expansions and the older frozen cost manifest; strict improvement is checked in the complete typed theorem class without scalar projection. Partition reuse applies during generation and independent replay.

`ApplyAt` refuses distribution point values; `Product` refuses delta-square and a multiplier proved discontinuous at the delta support. The corresponding public reasons are `DistributionPointValue`, `DistributionProductUndefined` and `TestSpaceMismatch`. Unsupported smoothness remains `PendingPremise`. The independent `delta_order`, `test_nodes` and cumulative `pairing_steps` limits cover these operations.

**Definition (Delta pullbacks and primitive uniqueness)** · `def:cas:distribution-pullbacks`

`Task::Pullback { source_space, target_space, map_kind }` consumes an original delta and a scalar map from target to source. `PullbackKind::Affine` checks a nonzero scale, the complete interval image and the inverse scalar expression in the source coordinate. The output uses the reciprocal absolute Jacobian: `δ(2x−1) = ½δ_(1/2)` and `δ(−3x+6) = ⅓δ_2`. Map syntax permits positive powers and division by nonzero constant polynomials; strict zero powers and hidden denominator holes refuse. Both spaces and both maps remain in `DistributionStatement::Pullback`.

`PullbackKind::SimpleZeros(roots)` requires the source zero delta on the whole real axis and a complete sorted rational-root list. The checker verifies the entire factorization, each root, each nonzero slope and its reciprocal absolute value. Thus `δ(x²−1) = ½δ_(-1) + ½δ_1`; a repeated root or omitted factor returns `PullbackHypotheses`. Roots outside the target interval remain in the certificate but contribute no delta. `pullback_certificate` exposes this checked evidence; `pullback_zeros` bounds all supplied roots before copying and replay allocation. General root solving and arbitrary nonlinear distribution pullbacks remain outside this fragment.

`Task::PrimitiveUnique { space }` checks equal first derivatives on a connected open interval. `DistributionStatement::PrimitiveUnique` asserts that the two distributions differ by some constant distribution; it does not select a constant or an initial condition. Step and step plus seven both differentiate to `δ_0`. The three new primitive keys are `dist.affine-pullback`, `dist.simple-zero-pullback` and `dist.primitive-unique`, at semantic version 1 and logical weights 12, 12 and 8. The complete default policy is version 9 with 77 registered schemas: the existing 59 executable checkers and 18 unavailable solving schemas.

**Definition (Construction capability)** · `def:cas:construction`

`Cas::admit` validates flat child-before-parent drafts and publishes them atomically. Handles carry a private store identity; public logical identifiers never authorize a lookup in another store. Graph-owned expression records share exact syntax, while each original operand use keeps a distinct occurrence, optional checked UTF-8 byte span and ordered child links. Sources with conflicting bytes under one key are refused. Failed admission preserves the published graph and all next identifiers.

`Operator` represents scalar arithmetic, declarations and calls, derivatives, integral/sum/series/limit bodies, predicates and solution sets, matrices and comprehensions, matrix operations, spectral requests and selected algebraic roots. Admission checks arity, sort, matrix shape and explicit de Bruijn frames. `ExpressionDraft::space` carries an optional exact typed link: ordinary pointwise scalar syntax requires absence, scalar-valued distribution actions retain their space, declared test/distribution leaves must match their declaration, and nonleaf space-dependent syntax requires an explicit matching space. Portable records and typed transformations retain that dependency. Representation supplies no evaluation, identity or proof of a predicate. Context keys sort declarations and named hypotheses, flatten conjunctions and reject recognized literal falsehoods or an atom paired with its explicit negation; unrecognized consistency remains unknown.

`Rational` owns canonical sign/magnitude, positive-denominator and reduction invariants over `num-bigint` 0.4 with default features disabled. Integers, fractions, decimals and decimal scientific notation parse exactly. Arithmetic cross-cancels before multiplication and uses continued fractions for comparison. The default component/intermediate limits are 4,096/8,192 bits; `(4/5)^78` fits with numerator/denominator sizes of 157/182 bits. Every big-integer operation prepays a conservative size-dependent work charge; a smaller bit budget returns `Exhausted` with `ArithmeticRange`. `petgraph` 0.8 supplies append-only graph storage. The gate-resolved lock pins `num-bigint` 0.4.8 and `petgraph` 0.8.3, including their exact transitive dependencies. This capability adds no dependency and preserves those lock bytes.

`source::parse_original(&mut cas, source_key, text, &declaration_header, &mut budget)` returns `Result<OriginalForm, ParseError<'_>>`. Obtain the declaration header directly from admission or through `Cas::context_header`. The source factory stages ordinary `Draft::Source`, `Draft::Expression` and `Draft::Occurrence` records and admits them together. `OriginalForm::syntax` is shared mathematical syntax; `OriginalForm::root` is the distinct original occurrence root. Every occurrence has its source key, precise byte span and ordered children. The caller still completes `walk_original` before making a request. The declared text grammar is:

```ebnf
expression     = sum ;
sum            = product, { ("+" | "-"), product } ;
product        = unary, { ("*" | "/"), unary } ;
unary          = ("+" | "-"), unary | power ;
power          = atom, [ "^", exponent ] ;
atom           = number | name, [ "(", [ sum, { ",", sum } ], ")" ]
               | "(", sum, ")" ;
exponent       = signed_integer | "(", signed_integer, [ "/", digits ], ")" ;
number         = digits, [ ".", digits ], [ ("e" | "E"), signed_integer ] ;
signed_integer = [ "+" | "-" ], digits ;
digits         = digit, { digit } ;
name           = (letter | "_"), { letter | digit | "_" } ;
digit          = "0" ... "9" ;
letter         = "A" ... "Z" | "a" ... "z" ;
```

ASCII whitespace may separate tokens; a number and each signed integer are contiguous. The complete input must match. Binary operators associate left; powers bind above unary signs and have exactly one literal exponent. A fraction denominator must be nonzero. Ordinary `1/2` retains `Div` and both original operands; decimals and scientific tokens become exact `Operator::Rational` values. A directly prefixed negative numeric token becomes a signed rational unless a power intervenes: `-2^2` retains `Neg(IntegerPower(2))`. Parentheses and unary plus widen the enclosed occurrence's span without adding an operator. Leading/trailing whitespace is outside the root span.

Names resolve by exact declaration key in the supplied admitted header, including inherited declarations; display names do not create aliases. Numeric literals use that header's scalar field, with no implicit sort coercion. Calls retain ordered arguments and their declared signature. Reduced exponents fitting `i64` use `IntegerPower`; all other exact rational exponents use `GeneralPower` with the header's explicit `Branch` and an original rational exponent operand. An integer exponent is operator payload, so it has no separate operand occurrence; a general exponent's occurrence span includes its optional parentheses.

`ParseError` borrows the caller's source key and any offending name, carries a half-open UTF-8 byte `span`, and reports the first unconsumed byte as `parsed_to`. `ParseFailure::Syntax` has stable `UnexpectedToken`, `UnclosedParenthesis`, `TrailingOperator`, `InvalidLiteral` and `InvalidExponent` codes. Missing closing delimiters identify the unmatched opening parenthesis; trailing operators identify the operator itself. `UnknownName`, `NotCallable` and `Arity` retain the identifier and its span; arity also reports expected and actual argument counts. `ParseFailure::Fault` preserves arithmetic, admission and exhaustion classifications unchanged. Refusal publishes no graph prefix or source key and does not refund consumed work.

Parsing shares the caller's `Budget`, intersected with store limits and restored on return. `Depth` bounds both grammar nesting (root depth one; each sign, group or call argument descends once) and resulting operand depth. `OriginalOccurrences` includes existing store occupancy and every newly staged use. `LiteralBytes` counts a numeric token, a directly folded minus, or the combined signed numerator, slash and denominator of a rational exponent, excluding separating whitespace and exponent parentheses. Source/name bytes, staged vertices/payload, arithmetic and work use the same existing limits. Explicit continuation frames avoid native-stack recursion even when the caller raises depth; temporary storage is conservatively charged cumulatively before allocation. No dependency or separate authoritative expression representation is added.

**Rule (Occurrence coverage before requests)** · `rule:cas:coverage`

`walk_original` visits every original operand in postorder, including operands beneath zero multiplication and cancellation candidates, with explicit binder-entry events. It returns a sealed `TraversalReceipt` only when the entire walk succeeds. A `WalkFailure` retains bounded partial events and the fault, with no receipt. A receipt proves coverage, not physical validity or mathematical definedness. A client must inspect the events and perform its own checks before submitting a request. `substitute` performs simultaneous capture-safe free substitution through the complete typed dependency graph; `instantiate_bound` replaces one explicit frame. Both return typed syntax, which must be materialized and walked again for a fresh receipt. `rename_axes` requires an `AxisMap` with exact coordinate, header and space correspondences; ordinary substitution cannot change a declared space or silently perform a coordinate pullback. `materialize_derived` produces fresh occurrences carrying provenance and no invented source spans, which `walk_derived` covers separately.

Walk work is charged as frames and events are produced, with each reached immutable interpretation record charged once per walk. Its record charge is at least twice that record's logical payload, so a walk reaching the entire store retains the former conservative full-store floor. Unrelated records contribute no work. Staged payload checks cover the walk's own live frames, retained events, dependency frontier, visited-record set and encoding scratch.

Draft admission, proof checking and replay, and producer transactions use one append-only rollback journal instead of copying the accumulated store. Each checkpoint restores node and edge indices, logical indexes and next identifiers, interning keys, policy identities, source keys, parent claims and all payload counters on refusal. An inner refusal undoes only its edits; an inner commit remains reversible by its enclosing transaction. Work covers touched payload, a fixed 256-unit counter checkpoint, logical key probes and validation, without a transaction charge proportional to unrelated records. Storage checks still include retained occupancy, pending drafts and journal reservation; nested transaction checkpoints reserve their bounded counter inventory under the existing staged-payload ceiling. Spent work is never refunded. Earlier witness bytes and future logical identifiers survive invalid input and mid-batch exhaustion unchanged. Full store copies are available only to tests needing an untouched control. Copying a checked application charges its own typed payload and optional scalar cache. Substitution prepays each reached record and copied scope, accumulating draft and memo workspace under the existing staged-payload limit; unrelated stored syntax contributes no work.

**Definition (Closed proof kernel)** · `def:cas:proof-kernel`

`Cas::check_proof(context, steps, budget)` checks a supplied child-before-parent proof DAG and returns a sealed `VerifiedWitness`. Its final `Judgment` names the full context, layer, optional space, typed statement and canonical guarded residuals. The closed `Primitive` vocabulary has 59 executable version-one schemas and 18 registered solving schemas. The executable families are: equality structure and ordered congruence, scalar field laws, exact rational arithmetic, finite polynomial/rational coefficient identities, elementary scalar derivative rules, equality transport, simultaneous substitution, ordered Jacobian assembly, certified square-free layers, `ctx.hypothesis`, `ctx.weaken`, `ctx.close`, `region.decide`, `region.construct`, `case.ordered`, `case.domain`, `case.select`, `case.congruence`, `case.eliminate`, `jet.polynomial`, `diff.case-interior`, `diff.case-boundary`, the twelve distribution schemas above, and the four split/germ schemas. The case certificates retain the complete reconstructed region and association inventory and exact cell evidence. `PRIMITIVES` exposes keys, semantic versions, conclusion/premise/certificate metadata and positive weights under the current `logical` policy version 9. Callers cannot register a trusted callback or label an arbitrary equation primitive. The schema matchers reconstruct conclusions and check each ordered premise; derivative relations cannot be reversed or composed as equality.

The graph owns checked Application and Proof records, their context/endpoints, ordered premise links and typed domain conditions. Scoped application edges reconstruct each statement's region, boundary, side, function and ordered value roles; unordered cover members use canonical local identity order. Admission is atomic, including failed proof imports and substitutions. A proof references only earlier proofs; unreachable or duplicate canonical proof records are rejected. `register_derived` replays a sealed witness in an independent empty store, compares its exact expected claim, and admits only a positive strict cost improvement over known routes for the same context/endpoints/relation/residual set. A shortcut retains its older proof dependency; it never replaces that evidence. The compiled registry stays closed and versioned. `register_derived_with_policy` replays an equality witness and requires strict improvement over the finite known equality routes under its complete pinned table; shortcuts registered under another policy cannot supply a stale improvement record.

**Rule (Visible domain evidence)** · `rule:cas:proof-domains`

The kernel derives requirements from both complete endpoints and every premise. Rational facts, exact named hypotheses, positivity implying nonzero, and nonzero products/integer powers discharge guards through bounded checks. Unknown requirements remain explicit `GuardedObligation` tuples naming home context, scope, layer, space, region and predicate; a refuted requirement blocks admission. The condition set acts as conjunction introduction, while conjunction hypotheses are projected with their original hypothesis key. Cancellation of x/x retains x nonzero; multiplication by zero retains the definedness of its other operand. Zero powers follow the strict nonzero-base convention, and the exponent-one derivative uses the child derivative directly. Simultaneous theorem substitution transports every requirement, including previously assumed ones, so an x nonzero hypothesis cannot justify substituting zero into x/x.

`is_discharged()` means no residual remains. `hypotheses()` still identifies every context assumption used, and `context_consistency()` remains `Unknown`. Open binder requirements retain their checked term scope; the scalar checker cannot turn one bound instance into a universal fact. No proof or witness supplies a physical validation receipt. Field identities follow the scalar field axioms; coefficient equality compares finite exact maps after reconstructing both endpoints, and rational equality retains original guards before cross multiplication. The mathematical arguments and derivative formulas are in `docs/design/cas-design.md`. Coefficient maps are temporary checked views of graph syntax, bounded by total degree, the union of variables across monomials, nonzero term count, measured scratch storage and exact arithmetic work.

**Definition (Portable mathematical witnesses)** · `def:cas:portable-witnesses`

`export_witness` writes only `NRZCAS03`: the used primitive manifest, canonical typed dependency records and complete conclusion. Headers, regions, spaces, expressions, arms, boundaries, source forms, predicates, contexts, guarded obligations, statements and applications have distinct record schemas. Kahn ordering chooses ready structural descriptors with already assigned child indices; unordered semantic sets use portable structural order. Runtime handles, graph serials, search costs and original receipts are absent. `NRZCAS01`, `NRZCAS02` and every other magic are refused as `UnsupportedVersion` before replay allocation. There are no external consumers or stored artifacts requiring a compatibility reader.

`replay(bytes, expected_judgment, budget)` reconstructs all mathematical tables through ordinary admission, checks every primitive and condition again, compares the complete canonical re-encoding, and compares the caller's expected full context, statement, domain, layer, space and guarded residual set. Expected terms cannot be reinterpreted under a different semantic header. `inspect` performs the same checking while deliberately reporting what the bytes prove without an acceptance target. A different valid theorem is rejected by `replay`; a different valid proof of the intended theorem is permitted. Truncated/extended streams, forward references, unused records, unknown rule versions, nonminimal numbers, changed guards and mismatched claims refuse without publishing a decoded prefix. The separate replay allowance covers decoding, construction, checking, publication and expected-claim comparison before work occurs.

**Procedure (Bounded identity search)** · `alg:cas:identity-search`

`Equal` and `Simplify` share two ordered queues, positive cost/progress quotas and a resumable finite rule/position cursor. The default epoch takes four cost visits and one progress visit. Matching, copying, container comparisons, arithmetic and conditions consume monotone ledgers. Structural expansion, exact coefficient collection and monomial cancellation propose ordinary proof steps; the kernel reconstructs every admitted edge. Existing proofs and policy-compatible shortcuts are expanded to their older primitive dependencies before reuse. The difference-quotient fixture requires an explicit larger intermediate before collection reaches its target, retaining the increment's nonzero guard. `Expand` materializes the bounded polynomial coefficient expression and checks the resulting finite-jet edge.

Each label retains cost, peak unfolded size, cumulative positive expansion credits, proof depth, witness footprint and proof/domain support. Dominance requires all resource dimensions to improve without increasing support, and never crosses context or residual classes. Exact ties use canonical proof bytes. The best result stays within the initial residual class; a policy requiring discharge cannot hide an initial unknown guard. An equality target must be reached exactly before a successful result is returned. An incomplete frontier or refused candidate never proves that no other route exists. These are heuristic routes, with no global shortest-path claim.

**Definition (Pinned logical costs)** · `def:cas:cost-policies`

`run_with_policy` accepts a `SearchPolicy` containing a complete positive `CostPolicy` table and conditional-result permission. Every immutable table is stored in the graph with its key, version and full primitive manifest. Reusing an identity/version with different contents is invalid. Default weights use `logical` version 9 with the unchanged 59 weights and the 18 fixed solving weights, sorted by ASCII key. Logical versions below 9 and modified logical v9 tables are rejected; custom policies use their own identity/version; custom weights are at most 1,000,000. Logical transition cost and expanded primitive count are reported separately. `certify_frozen_cost` projects only checked equality applications in the exact context/residual class, pins its manifest and table, and settles every reachable endpoint by bounded positive-weight Dijkstra. Its sealed certificate states a shortest cost or finite-snapshot unreachability. New applications affect only a new manifest; exhaustion yields no certificate. Raw operands, source links and proof-dependency links are never cost edges.

**Procedure (Generated scalar calculus)** · `alg:cas:generated-calculus`

Differentiation uses an explicit postorder stack and memoizes by expression, context and ordered variable key. It proposes constant, variable, negation, sum, difference, product, quotient and fixed-integer-power formulas. Simplification requires checked rational identities and derivative transport. Composite denominators retain factored syntax when coefficient expansion would introduce additional unknown guards. Zero and negative powers retain nonzero conditions; exponent one avoids a new zero power. Unknown functions and other unimplemented grammar produce an unresolved blocker naming the pending logical expression. No whole Jacobian is emitted until all ordered partials check. Shared expressions reuse proof work while the requested row and variable order remain explicit.

The three-output pendulum fixture checks a three-by-eight Jacobian, the shared final two columns and exact nominal coefficients. Its simultaneous operating-point substitutions are also applied to original domain judgments with the equality-instantiation schema. Independent hand formulas give the nonzero cells pi², -(621/625)pi² and -(1242/625)pi². Neither those symbolic derivatives nor their substitutions infer a covariance model or physical validity.

A request uses distinct generation and checker work ledgers; every candidate check shares the same checker allowance. A best result additionally undergoes fresh-store canonical byte replay before return. Higher derivative levels replay the necessary proof roots, whose checked dependency closures also certify their retained subproofs. An unrelated nonexistence fact needs its own replayed root; all replay work shares the original request allowance. Each ledger memoizes successful exact region partitions by complete portable syntax, ordered roots, the optional boundary cut and active ceilings. Fresh receiving stores can reuse those decisions; every supplied certificate and asserted relation is still checked. The cache reserves staged payload, and key construction, lookup and copying remain metered. `SearchResult` reports both usages, its exact endpoint trace and full policy. Its optional `NRZRUN03` envelope separates costs, effective limits, counters and trace from the authoritative `NRZCAS03` witnesses, including every retained calculus component. If the optional response cannot fit, `response` is absent (NotExported) while the sealed in-store witness remains available for a later metered export. Minimal outcome metadata does not require optional output capacity.

**Procedure (Certified square-free layers)** · `alg:cas:square-free-layers`

`SquareFree` reconstructs a nonzero univariate polynomial over Q from scalar rational/real syntax, normalizes its leading coefficient, differentiates its coefficient vector and computes exact extended Euclidean certificates. Successive gcd/quotient layers follow the characteristic-zero recurrence in `docs/design/cas-design.md`. Each certificate states both divisibilities and a Bézout combination; the checker verifies those coefficient identities without running the producer's Euclidean loop. It also verifies the recurrence, monicity, nonconstant factors, strictly increasing positive multiplicities, each factor's derivative gcd, every pairwise gcd, the complete product identity and the exact displayed endpoint factors. Layers need not be irreducible; x²+1 remains a valid single layer over Q.

`SquareFreeCertificate`, `SquareFreeLayer` and `GcdCertificate` are untrusted public data with ascending-degree canonical rational vectors. `ApplicationDraft::certificate` uses `Certificate::SquareFree(Box<SquareFreeCertificate>)` only for `poly.square-free` version 1, with logical weight 8; other primitives reject that payload. `square_free_certificate` borrows checked evidence from the witness's graph application. The typed application contains the complete certificate; no earlier wire layout is retained. Every decoded coefficient/vector and retained certificate is bounded before copying or arithmetic. The producer publishes the factor expression and its replayed proof in one transaction.

A nonzero constant returns its scalar and no factors. Zero returns `Outcome::Unsupported` with `UnsupportedReason::ZeroPolynomialMultiplicity`; non-rational coefficients and expressions outside the univariate fragment have distinct reasons. Positive-characteristic and complex coefficient requests remain unsupported. Degree, coefficient/intermediate bits, arithmetic work, retained payload and witness bytes have explicit refusal paths. The degree-five fixture, a hand-authored Bézout certificate for x²+1, independent binomial examples, rational coefficients, endpoint/certificate tampering, canonical replay, byte determinism and exact witness-size boundary are acceptance tests. None of these claims infer roots or physical meaning.

**Caveat (Current executable scope)** · `cav:cas:construction-scope`

`Cas::run` executes bounded `Equal`, `Simplify`, `Expand`, `Differentiate`, `Jacobian`, `SquareFree`, `ElaborateCases`, `Select`, `Eliminate`, `Merge`, `Coverage`, `Integrate`, `Sum`, `Limit`, `Series`, `EmbedRegular`, `Pair`, polynomial `SmoothMultiply` and boundary ownership, jet, jump and explicitly ordered point derivative requests after validating complete matching occurrence receipts. `Proved` and `Conditional` contain independently checked results; the latter retains unresolved domain guards. Contradicted definedness and a proved empty requested function domain return `Refused` with `RefutedDomain` or `EmptyDomain`. Native unsupported premises return `PendingPremise`, and undecided coverage returns `UnknownTotality`; both carry any independently replayed partial facts. A checked point obstruction returns `Nonexistent`. Mixed whole-domain derivative results return `Partial` with a sealed `DerivativeMap`. Other represented tasks remain `Unresolved`, and excluded executable fields return `Unsupported`. Malformed inputs return `Invalid`; spent limits return `Exhausted` with only a previously replayed best result when available, stored in `Option<Box<SearchResult>>`. `canonical_snapshot` is a deterministic graph diagnostic, not a mathematical witness or import format.

`Limits` names every storage, depth, bit, work and future algorithm ceiling; `LimitsOverride` resolves optional request changes. A `Budget` is an explicit monotone ledger. Every active limit, including work and replay work, is the componentwise minimum of the requested/default allowance and the permanent store ceiling; a request cannot raise that ceiling. Transactions roll back failed graph edits. Conservative estimates may refuse before the nominal vertex or bit cap is reached. Allocator failure is returned where the underlying API offers fallible reservation; environmental allocator abort behavior has the limitation stated in `docs/design/cas-design.md`.

